<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Huan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sanhuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Huan were the Jisun, Mengsun and Shusun, three ministerial families of the State of Lu descended from sons of Duke Huan of Lu. From the sixth century BCE they controlled most of Lu's land and armies, dividing the state's forces among themselves and reducing the dukes to figureheads. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, who served in Lu, tried without success to curb their power by dismantling their fortified towns.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The three houses were cadet branches of the ducal house of Lu, itself a branch of the royal house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. Duke Huan of Lu, who reigned from 711 to 694 BCE, had four sons. The eldest by his main wife Wen Jiang succeeded him as Duke Zhuang of Lu, while the other three, Qingfu, Shuya and Jiyou, became powerful officials under their brother.</p><p>Their family names came from the ordinals used in courtesy names to mark a person's place among brothers: Meng for an eldest son born to a secondary wife, Shu for a middle son and Ji for the youngest. As the power of these lines became hereditary, the descendants of Duke Zhuang's brothers took the ordinals as surnames to tell their branches of the House of Ji apart, giving the Mengsun, Shusun and Jisun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Their hold over Lu began with a succession crisis. The dying Duke Zhuang wanted his son Ziban to succeed; Shuya backed Qingfu and was poisoned by Jiyou, who supported Ziban. Ziban was soon murdered by Qingfu with the help of Duke Zhuang's wife, and Jiyou went into exile. In 660 BCE Qingfu also murdered Duke Min and tried to rule himself, but public outcry drove him to the state of Ju. Jiyou returned from Qi with Duke Min's younger brother, who became Duke Xi, Qingfu was forced to take his own life, and Jiyou served as chief minister for 16 years with the backing of Qi.</p><p>Rivalry among the three families shaped the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s time. His disciples Ran Yong, Ran Qiu and Zilu served the Jisun as ministers, and Confucius himself was a subordinate of Ji Huanzi, chancellor to Duke Ding of Lu. He is said to have taken part in efforts to weaken the families and strengthen the ruler, but he was ousted from Lu in 497 BCE and remained in exile until 484 BCE.</p><p>At the end of the Spring and Autumn period Duke Ai of Lu, who had no control of his own army, secretly asked the powerful state of Yue to invade, depose the Three Huan and restore the duke's power. The plot was discovered and Duke Ai fled abroad. His son and successor, Duke Dao, remained a puppet of the three houses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Three Huan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power slipped from the dukes to three ministerial families, the <b>Jisun</b>, Mengsun and Shusun.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-lu">State of Lu</a></li></ul></section>
